  "story": {
    "id": "sofia_school_run",
    "person_id": "sofia_reyes",
    "title": "School run, three agents",
    "summary": "Sofia walks her child to school through a plaza where a delivery bot, pedestrians, and a curious five-year-old all share one narrow strip.",
    "beats": [
      {
        "id": "setup",
        "text": "Sofia buckles the stroller strap and takes Luca's hand. The plaza path to school is shorter than the avenue; today it is also busier. She has eight minutes and a coffee she will not get to drink.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "approach",
        "text": "They join the morning flow: parents, bags, a courier cutting through. Luca points at every wheel that is not a car. Sofia steers for the wider edge of the plaza strip.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "encounter",
        "text": "A delivery bot pauses at a bench cluster. Luca pulls toward it — \"it has eyes!\" — while a surge of pedestrians squeezes the remaining sidewalk. Sofia's stroller wheel clips the bot's bumper with a soft plastic knock.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "reaction",
        "text": "She apologizes out of habit, steers Luca back, and waits for a gap. The bot does nothing hostile; it also does nothing helpful. Sofia feels neither fear nor fondness — only the math of minutes, width, and a child who treats the machine like a puppy.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "outcome",
        "text": "They arrive at school on time. Sofia files the moment as \"fine, if crowded.\" She would not ban the robots; she would like them to leave a clear human lane during school peaks, and to keep moving when kids stop to stare.\n"
      }
    ],
